# ==========================================================================
#  O63  OQuaRE in a page
# ==========================================================================
#
#  DECIDES
#    Quality is assessed with a published model rather than a feeling:
#    OQuaRE's characteristics, sub-characteristics and metrics, each metric
#    scored one to five against the paper's thresholds, and the scores read
#    as a map of strengths rather than a mark.
#
#  WHY
#    OQuaRE takes the software quality standard SQuaRE, ISO/IEC 25000, and
#    adapts it to ontologies. The shape is three layers. Characteristics at
#    the top: structural, functional adequacy, reliability, operability,
#    maintainability, compatibility, transferability, quality in use. Sub-
#    characteristics under each: cohesion, tangledness, redundancy and
#    consistency under structural; modularity, reusability, analysability,
#    changeability under maintainability; the intended uses of an ontology,
#    from controlled vocabulary to inferencing, under functional adequacy.
#    And metrics at the bottom, fourteen of them, most adapted from object-
#    oriented software metrics: depth of inheritance, number of children,
#    coupling, cohesion, richness of attributes, relations and annotations.
#
#    Each metric is scored one to five, where one is not acceptable, three
#    minimally acceptable and five exceeds requirements, against thresholds
#    the paper proposes. A sub-characteristic's score is the mean of its
#    metrics; a characteristic's is the mean of its sub-characteristics.
#    The authors are careful to say that the result is meant to show
#    strengths and weaknesses, not to rank ontologies, and that the
#    thresholds are proposals from software practice awaiting validation.
#    Both cautions are worth keeping.
#
#    The metrics are computed in this course by scripts/metrics.py, which
#    follows the paper's formulas and states its own choices where the
#    paper is silent. The measured line has the Trail's scores; o64 and o65
#    read them, and o68 says what they miss.
#
#  DIAGRAM
#       SQuaRE (ISO/IEC 25000)  ->  OQuaRE
#
#       characteristic      sub-characteristics                         metrics (examples)
#       structural          formalisation, cohesion, tangledness, ...   LCOMOnto, TMOnto, DITOnto
#       functional adequacy controlled vocabulary, reasoning, ...       RROnto, AROnto, INROnto, ANOnto, CROnto
#       maintainability     modularity, reusability, analysability, ... WMCOnto, DITOnto, NOCOnto, RFCOnto, NOMOnto, CBOOnto
#       reliability, operability, compatibility, transferability, quality in use
#
#       score 1..5 per metric, by threshold; means upward; strengths and weaknesses, not a rank
#
#  WHAT TO TAKE AWAY
#    - A quality model is three layers: characteristics, sub-
#      characteristics, metrics. The metrics are the only part a script
#      computes.
#    - Scores are one to five against proposed thresholds. Read them as a
#      map, not a mark.
#    - The authors' own caveat: the thresholds came from software
#      practice and await validation. Keep it in mind.
#
#  CORNER    thought
#  DATA      01-vocabulary.ttl
#  RUNS IN   the browser; the scores come from scripts/metrics.py on data/01-vocabulary.ttl
#  LOAD IT   https://semantechs.co.uk/turtle-editor-viewer/?dot=https%3A%2F%2Fraw.githubusercontent.com%2Fpwin%2FOntology_Course%2Fmain%2Flessons%2F10-quality%2Fo63-oquare-in-a-page.ttl
#  SOURCES   Duque-Ramos, Fernandez-Breis, Stevens and Aussenac-Gilles, OQuaRE: a SQuaRE-based approach for evaluating the quality of ontologies, JRPIT 43(2), 2011
#            http://www.cs.man.ac.uk/~stevensr/papers/OQuareProof.pdf
#            The Ontology Quality Suite
#            https://github.com/pwin/consolidated-ontology-quality-suite-python
#  MEASURED  parses, 6 triples; OQuaRE DIT 2 (5), ANOnto 2.00 (5)
# ==========================================================================

@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .

skos:prefLabel      a owl:AnnotationProperty .
skos:definition     a owl:AnnotationProperty .
skos:editorialNote  a owl:AnnotationProperty .

<https://example.org/bookshop-trail/schema>
    a                   owl:Ontology ;
    skos:prefLabel      "The Bookshop Trail schema"@en ;
    skos:editorialNote  "Assessed with OQuaRE (Duque-Ramos et al., 2011). Scores are in the course document beside this lesson; the script that computed them is scripts/metrics.py."@en .
